window.STORY_ADDONS_TW_EAST.korea[2]={id:'korea-5',title:'處容的歌舞與門上的畫像',tag:'新羅傳說與禳疫',summary:'《三國遺事》記述處容從海上龍王的家族來到新羅。面對闖入家中的疫神，他以歌舞回應，對方於是許諾避開他的形象，門上畫像的護佑作用由此獲得來歷。',paragraphs:[
'《三國遺事》的新羅故事把處容的來歷放在憲康王時代。國王在東海岸遇到雲霧遮路，經詢問與安排佛事之後，海上龍王顯現，帶著七個兒子奏樂起舞。其中一子處容隨王進入都城，得到官職併成婚。龍王、地方景觀與王權因而進入同一段文學記述。',
'後來，疫神迷戀處容的妻子，化作人形來到他們家中。處容夜間歸來，見床上有四條腿，便唱出關於眼前情景的歌，並起舞退出。他沒有照英雄降妖的慣常期待立即舉兵搏鬥。這裡的歌辭與行為在後世產生多種解釋，不宜把一次複述直接當作對婚姻關係的現代道德判詞。',
'疫神現形致歉，稱處容沒有因受冒犯而發怒，自己因此感動，許諾以後看見他的畫像就不進入。人們遂把處容影像放在門上以求禳疫。後世處容舞有自己的宮廷表演與傳承歷史，不是把故事一成不變地重演；這篇記述也不能代替對古代疾病、醫療與儀式的歷史研究。'],en:{title:'Cheoyong’s Dance and the Image at the Door',tag:'Silla legend & protection',summary:'In the Samguk yusa, Cheoyong comes from the sea dragon’s family to Silla. Confronting an epidemic spirit at home, he sings and dances. The spirit promises to avoid his image, giving protective doorway pictures a narrative origin.',paragraphs:[
'The Samguk yusa places Cheoyong’s arrival in the reign of King Heongang of Silla. Mist obstructs the king’s route near the eastern coast. After inquiries and arrangements for Buddhist activity, a sea dragon appears with seven sons who perform music and dance. One son, Cheoyong, accompanies the king to the capital, receives office, and marries, connecting landscape, sacred ancestry, and royal authority.',
'An epidemic spirit later desires Cheoyong’s wife and enters their house in human form. Returning at night, Cheoyong sees four legs in the bed, sings of what he finds, and withdraws dancing instead of immediately attacking. His song and conduct have generated differing interpretations. A short retelling should not turn this literary encounter into a simple modern judgment about marriage or a definitive account of his feelings.',
'The spirit reveals itself and apologizes, moved that Cheoyong did not respond with anger. It promises not to enter wherever his image appears, explaining the use of doorway pictures for protection against epidemic forces. Later Cheoyong dance has a distinct history of court performance and transmission, not a mechanically unchanged reenactment. The narrative does not replace historical research into illness, medicine, or ritual.']}};

window.STORY_ADDONS_TW={...window.STORY_ADDONS_TW_EAST,...window.STORY_ADDONS_TW_WEST,...window.STORY_ADDONS_TW_WORLD};
